<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Port of document_processing/ — the library. Must NOT reference the service project: that is
    rule 10 of service/ml/runtime.py ("only this module imports the library") turned into a
    compile-time fact, and it is what lets the service be tested without 215 MB of models.
    An architecture test asserts it, because a project reference is one IDE click away.

    TargetFramework, Nullable and ImplicitUsings come from Directory.Build.props — repeating them
    here is how two projects quietly end up on different settings.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The GPU package ALONE, and this is a decision rather than an oversight.

      Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.Gpu contains the CPU execution provider as well, so ONE build
      serves both devices — which is what the deployment needs ("GPU if available, else CPU") and
      what the Python service already does with a single onnxruntime-gpu wheel. Referencing both
      packages would place two copies of the native runtime in the output and let the loader pick
      unpredictably.

      This is where .NET differs usefully from Go: Go links one specific libonnxruntime, so the
      Go port needs two Docker images. .NET needs one.

      A host without CUDA is not an error — the runtime falls back, and the device-selection
      logic reports the provider it actually got rather than the ones merely listed.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.Gpu" />

    <PackageReference Include="OpenCvSharp4" />
    <!--
      Native OpenCV, per platform. Conditioned so a Linux build does not carry Windows DLLs and
      vice versa. Both are pinned to the same four-part version as the managed wrapper: the two
      halves of OpenCvSharp are released together, and a mismatch fails at load time with a
      message about a missing entry point rather than about versions.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="OpenCvSharp4.runtime.win" Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('Windows'))" />
    <PackageReference Include="OpenCvSharp4.official.runtime.linux-x64" Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('Linux'))" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
